4. ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW
================================================================================

The platform is designed in three cohesive layers:

 [ User Workstation / Browser / llama.cpp Web UI ]
                         │
                         ▼
           ┌───────────────────────────┐
           │      GATEWAY (Proxy)      │  (Port: 8081)
           │   app.gateway:app         │
           └─────────────┬─────────────┘
                         │
             ┌───────────┴────────────────────────────────────────┐
             │ (Chat Completions:                                 │ (All Other Requests:
             │  /v1/chat/completions)                             │  WebUI assets, /props,
             ▼                                                    │  /v1/models, SSE)
       ┌───────────────────────────┐                              │
       │ FASTAPI INTELLIGENCE APP  │  (Port: 8000)                │
       │ app.main:app              │                              │
       ├───────────────────────────┤                              │
       │ • Input Type Detector     │                              │
       │   (Vision & Scanned PDFs) │                              │
       │ • Task Classifier LLM     │                              │
       │   (Coding/Reasoning/Gen)  │                              │
       │ • Capability Router       │                              │
       │   (models.yaml resolver)  │                              │
       └─────────────┬─────────────┘                              │
                     │                                            │
                     │ (Selected Model Request)                   │
                     ▼                                            │
             ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
             ▼
       ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
       │                  LLAMA.CPP SERVER                        │  (Port: 8080)
       │ llama-server with models.ini presets                     │
       ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
       │ • Multi-model hosting (GGUF format)                      │
       │ • Multi-GPU tensor/layer distribution (CUDA/Metal)       │
       │ • Built-in Web UI & OpenAI-compatible endpoints         │
       └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘


================================================================================
5. SYSTEM COMPONENTS EXPLAINED
================================================================================

1. Gateway Proxy (backend/app/gateway.py)
   - Listens on port 8081 as the unified public interface.
   - Forwards static web assets, llama.cpp GUI resources, and administrative
     endpoints (/v1/models, /props, SSE) directly to llama-server.
   - Transparently intercepts '/v1/chat/completions' and sends requests to the
     FastAPI intelligence layer for classification and dynamic routing.
   - Streams raw token responses back to the client with low latency.

2. FastAPI Intelligence Backend (backend/app/main.py)
   - Listens on port 8000 as the orchestration brain.
   - Input Detection (app/input/detector.py): Inspects payload messages for
     multimodal data (e.g. image_url blocks, base64 data, or embedded PDF text).
     If found, immediately routes to the 'document_image' model.
   - Task Classifier (app/services/task_analyzer.py): Employs a rapid local
     classification model to categorize prompts strictly into 'coding',
     'reasoning', or 'general'.
   - Model Router (app/services/model_router.py): Matches the identified task
     against configured capabilities and active models declared in models.yaml.
   - Model Service (app/services/model_services.py & app/inference/llama_client.py):
     Dispatches requests to llama-server using the resolved model alias and
     supports streaming generator yields.

3. llama.cpp Server (llama-server)
   - High-performance, memory-efficient C++ local LLM server.
   - Advanced GGUF Model Quantization: Runs large 70B+ parameter models
     efficiently on enterprise GPU/CPU hardware using optimal k-quants
     (e.g., Q4_K_M, Q5_K_M), minimizing VRAM requirements with negligible
     quality degradation.
   - Speculative Decoding Support: Drastically accelerates token generation
     throughput and cuts latency by utilizing a small draft model to predict
     tokens verified in parallel by the primary model.
   - Manages multiple concurrent model weights via preset files (models.ini).
   - Distributes layers across available hardware (GPUs/CPU via CUDA/Metal),
     supports Jinja prompt templating, and hosts the built-in browser UI.
   - Features pre-included Model Context Protocol (MCP) proxy support via
     '--webui-mcp-proxy' for standardizing local tool connectivity and agent
     execution.


================================================================================
6. IMPORTANT HARDWARE & DEPLOYMENT NOTICE
================================================================================

[!] WARNING: MULTI-GPU SERVER ENVIRONMENT
This system was designed, configured, and tested on a dedicated multi-GPU
production server with multiple discrete NVIDIA GPUs (e.g., devices 0 and 2).

DO NOT run the multi-GPU command with CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES="0,2" on your local
machine unless:
1. You have multiple physical NVIDIA GPUs installed.
2. The GPU indices specified exist on your host system.

If running locally on a single GPU, CPU, or Apple Silicon (Metal), use the
standard local example commands described in Section 9.


================================================================================
7. GETTING STARTED
================================================================================

Prerequisites:
- Python >= 3.13
- uv package manager (https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)
- llama.cpp compiled binary (llama-server)
- Downloaded GGUF model files placed in the models/ directory

Step 1: Create a Virtual Environment
-----------------------------------
From the project root:

    uv venv

Step 2: Activate the Virtual Environment
---------------------------------------
On macOS / Linux:

    source .venv/bin/activate

On Windows (Command Prompt / PowerShell):

    .venv\Scripts\activate

Step 3: Install Dependencies
----------------------------
Synchronize all project dependencies via uv:

    uv sync


================================================================================
8. CONFIGURATION (.EXAMPLE FILES EXPLAINED)
================================================================================

The repository provides template configuration files that must be adapted for
your specific deployment.

1. Environment Configuration: .env.example -> .env
--------------------------------------------------
Copy .env.example to .env in the project root:

    cp .env.example .env

Variables to configure:
- BACKEND_HOST / BACKEND_PORT:
  Host and port where FastAPI intelligence app runs (default: 127.0.0.1:8000).
- LLAMA_HOST / LLAMA_PORT:
  Host and port where llama-server runs (default: 127.0.0.1:8080).
- GATEWAY_HOST / GATEWAY_PORT:
  Host and port for the user-facing gateway proxy (default: 127.0.0.1:8081).

For server deployments, bind to your network interface or LAN IP
(e.g., 100.72.139.99).


2. Model Routing Configuration: models.example.yaml -> models.yaml
------------------------------------------------------------------
Copy backend/app/config/models.example.yaml to backend/app/config/models.yaml:

    cp backend/app/config/models.example.yaml backend/app/config/models.yaml

(models.yaml is gitignored to protect sensitive and host-specific configurations.)

Sections to customize:
- models.coding.name: Model alias for coding queries (e.g., "qwen3-4B").
- models.reasoning.name: Model alias for reasoning/analysis (e.g., "gemma4-12B").
- models.general.name: Fallback model for general conversation.
- models.document_image.name: Multimodal vision model for processing images/PDFs.
- task_analyzer.name: Lightweight model alias used strictly for classifying tasks.


3. llama.cpp Model Presets: models.ini
---------------------------------------
File: models.ini in the project root.
Configures GPU distribution and preloading for each model:
- [*]: Global settings (e.g., n-gpu-layers = 999 to maximize GPU offload).
- [<model-alias>]: Model blocks:
  - main-gpu: Assigns which physical GPU card holds model KV context.
  - load-on-startup: Loads model into VRAM on startup to eliminate cold starts.


4. Model Weights Directory: models/
-----------------------------------
File: models/README
Model weights (.gguf) are stored locally in the models/ directory and are
never committed to Git. Download compatible GGUF files and place them here.


================================================================================
9. RUNNING THE SYSTEM (COMMANDS & REFERENCE)
================================================================================

Three services run simultaneously to form the complete workbench. Start each in
a separate terminal or as a managed system service.

SERVICE 1: LLAMA.CPP INFERENCE SERVER (llama-server)
----------------------------------------------------

A) Real Server Multi-GPU Production Command:
   (Use ONLY on multi-GPU server environments with CUDA configured)

   CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES="GPU_NUM,GPU_NUM,...GPU_NUM" LD_LIBRARY_PATH=~/projects/snap-ai/llama.cpp/build/bin \
   ~/projects/snap-ai/llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-server \
     --models-dir ~/projects/snap-ai/models \
     --models-preset ~/projects/snap-ai/models.ini \
     --models-max 3 \
     --host 100.72.139.99 \
     --port 8080 \
     --parallel 1 \
     --jinja \
     --webui-mcp-proxy

B) Local / Single-GPU / CPU Example Command:
   (For local development or single-GPU workstations)

   ./llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-server \
     --models-dir ./models \
     --models-preset ./models.ini \
     --models-max 3 \
     --host 127.0.0.1 \
     --port 8080 \
     --parallel 1 \
     --jinja


SERVICE 2: FASTAPI INTELLIGENCE BACKEND (app.main:app)
------------------------------------------------------
From the backend directory:

A) Production Server Command:
   cd backend && uvicorn app.main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000

B) Local Development Command (with hot-reload):
   cd backend && uvicorn app.main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --reload


SERVICE 3: GATEWAY PROXY (app.gateway:app)
------------------------------------------
From the backend directory:

A) Production Server Command:
   cd backend && uvicorn app.gateway:app --host 100.72.139.99 --port 8081

B) Local Development Command:
   cd backend && uvicorn app.gateway:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8081 --reload


WEB UI ACCESS
-------------
Once the services are active, access the workbench from your browser:

- Production Server: http://100.72.139.99:8081
- Local Workstation: http://127.0.0.1:8081

Users interact with the familiar llama.cpp interface, while the backend
intelligently routes tasks behind the scenes.


================================================================================
10. END-TO-END REQUEST FLOW
================================================================================

1. User submits a request (prompt, image, or document) via the Web UI (:8081).
2. Gateway intercepts the POST to /v1/chat/completions.
3. Gateway passes the payload to the FastAPI Intelligence Layer (:8000).
4. FastAPI evaluates the input:
   - If an image or PDF attachment is detected -> Dispatches to 'document_image'.
   - If plain text -> Task Classifier runs a quick prompt against a local
     classifier model to categorize as 'coding', 'reasoning', or 'general'.
5. Model Router queries models.yaml to select the designated, enabled model.
6. Model Service issues the inference query to llama-server (:8080).
7. llama-server generates the response with GPU acceleration and streams
   tokens back through FastAPI -> Gateway -> Browser in real-time.
================================================================================
